\section*{A feedback-set bound for second neighborhoods}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Seymour second neighborhoods}: The paper claims that every finite nonempty oriented graph has a vertex whose second out-neighborhood is at least as large as its first. The second neighborhood excludes vertices already reachable in one step.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Seymour second neighborhoods}: A smallest counterexample is pruned to yield strict neighborhood inequalities for all proper vertex sets. Replacing vertices by transitive tournaments strengthens these to a discrete concavity inequality. Extremal families of ordered pairs and an augmented bipartite matching are then controlled by two factorizations of one matrix, contradicting that inequality.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Seymour second neighborhoods}: The paper claims that every finite nonempty oriented graph has a vertex whose second out-neighborhood is at least as large as its first. The second neighborhood excludes vertices already reachable in one step. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 173 describes Lean coverage for the exact second-out-neighborhood inequality for a suitable vertex of every finite nonempty oriented graph.

\textit{Seymour second neighborhoods}: The manuscript links \texttt{SeymourSecondNeighborhood.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.exists\_goodVertex}).

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

